Why C Language?
C is a powerful, simple, and portable programming language. It is called a middle-level language
 because it combines the features of both high-level and low-level languages. C is widely used for
  system programming, especially in developing operating systems. It provides rich libraries, 
  built-in operators, and supports procedural programming using functions. Its simplicity and ability 
  to manage memory manually (allocating and deallocating) make it suitable for performance-critical 
  applications. C is also easy to extend and provides a strong foundation for learning other programming 
  languages.

Applications of C:
C is used in designing operating systems, embedded systems in electronic gadgets, and GUI-based 
applications. It is the core of many popular systems such as the Google File System, Mozilla Firefox,
and MySQL. C's efficiency and control make it ideal for hardware-level programming and real-time systems.

Advantages of C:
C is simple, low-cost, portable, and provides fast execution speed. Being a middle-level language, 
it gives the programmer better control over the system and hardware.

Disadvantages of C:
C lacks features like object-oriented programming (OOP), runtime checking, and exception handling.
 It does not have constructors, destructors, or built-in garbage collection, which means memory must
be managed manually.